            </sidebyside>
        </figure>
    </activity>

    <p>
        Your book does a proof that two of these sets are equal on pages
        114-115.
        Let's sketch the other one.
    </p>

    <theorem workspace="6.2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are subsets of a universal set
                <m>U</m>.
                Then
                <md>
                    (A\cap B)^c = A^c \cup B^c.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Showing Disjointness</title>

        <p>
            Roughly, two sets are disjoint if they have no elements in common.
            The official definition is given in <xref ref="def-disjoint"/>.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-disjoint">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Two sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <term>disjoint</term>
                    provided that <m>A\cap B = \emptyset</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            Typically, if we want to prove two sets are disjoint we will use a
            proof technique called <q>proof by contradiction</q>.
            It seems hard to prove that something is equal to the empty set.
            Instead, we'll assume that <m>A\cap B \neq \emptyset</m>.
            This gives an element <m>x\in A\cap B</m>.
            We work from here to a contradiction, or something that can't
            possibly be true, so the original assumption must have been wrong.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-disjoint-congruence">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A = \{x\in\mathbb{Z} \mid x \equiv 2 \pmod{4} \}</m> and
                <m>B = \{y\in\mathbb{Z} \mid y\equiv 3 \pmod{6}\}</m> then
                <m>A\cap B = \emptyset</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <activity workspace = "2in">
      <p>
        Prove <xref ref="thm-disjoint-congruence"/>.
      </p>
    </activity>
</handout>